#!/usr/bin/bash

# Pacbio sequences and Enzymatic methyl sequences were obtained from ICB, BOKU.
# After these files where obtained, the Pacbio Seq was firts analysed using the procedure below:

# Methylation analysis of the PacBio Sequence 
# Raw Pacbio Hifi reads in BAM format were first filtered to collect only double-stranded reads 
# for downstream processes. Double-starnded reads were collected because at the time of this analysis, 
# the packages available couldn't work with single-stranded reads. This was done using the line of code below; 
   
samtools view -d "fi" -b TableBeet.Kinetics.CCS.hifi.bam > TableBeet.Kinetics.CCS.hifi.ds.bam

# Next, I applied the Primrose software (Version 1.3.0) to the retrieved double-stranded Hifi read data 
to predict the 5mC probabilities using their respective kinetic information with the line of code below;

primrose --min-passes 3 TKC_hifi.ds.bam TKC.hifi.ds.out.bam -j 20

# Next, the obtained HiFi reads containing 5mC tags (provided in an unaligned BAM format) were aligned to a 
tablebeet reference using pbmm2 version 1.12.0 (Pacific Bioscience, 2023) Using the line of code below;

pbmm2 align TB-assembly.posscaf.fa TKC.hifi.ds.out.bam TKC.hifi.ds.out.align.bam -j 30

# Then the indexed bam files were used to obtain Pileup scores for 5mC across CpG sites using the Pb_CpG_tool 
(version 2.3.1) with the line of code below;

aligned_bam_to_cpg_scores -b hifi.sort.bam -f TB-assembly.posscaf.fa -o pb -p count -m reference -t 30

# Then, I collected methylation information from the first 9 scaffolds, which is known to contain the most important 
sequences of the table beet genome, with coverage of ≥ 4 and methylation probability ≥20% using the line of code below;

awk '$1 ~ /^scaffold_[1-9]$/' pb.combined.reference.mincov4.bed | awk '$5>=20 && $6>=4' | awk	'{OFS="\t"} {print $1"\t"$2"\t"$3"\t"$4"\t"$6}' > mCG_pb1-9.bed


# Methylation analysis of the Pacbio_seq data Using the CCS_meth pipeline.
# The CCS_meth pipeline used in this study is based on a deep learning approach and was designed to detect methylation 
# in the 5mCpG context from circular consensus sequences (Hifi reads), it uses an attention-based bidirectional Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU) 
# networks to predict DNA methylation states of cytosine in Hifi reads (Peng N. et al., 2022). The approach also consisted of a series of 
# steps which is detailed in Figure 7B. and in the following lines of codes below;

# Modification probability calling from the aligned PacBio reads using the code below;

ccsmeth call_mods --input hifi.pbmm2.bam --ref ../TB-assembly.posscaf.fa \
--model_file model_ccsmeth_5mCpG_call_mods_attbigru2s_b21.v1.ckpt \
--output ./new/cmod --threads 50 --threads_call 10 --model_type attbigru2s \
--rm_per_readsite --mode align --modbam modbam --motif CG

# Extraction of modification probabilities for CG methylation context using the line of code below;

ccsmeth call_freqt --input_path /path/to/cmod.per_redsite.tsv \ 
	--result_file /path/to/new_CG_ccsmeth --bed –sort\ 
	--prob_cf 0.0 --refsites_only \
	--motifs CG --mod_loc 0 --ref /path/to/TB-assembly.posscaf.fa \
	--contigs --threads 30

# Next, the command line below using awk was used for extracting and sorting the necessary columns 
# and information, see the line of code below;
awk '{OFS="\t"} {print $1"\t"$2"\t"$3"\t"$6"\t"$11"\t"$10}' new_CG_ccsmeth | awk '$4=="+"{print $1"\t"$2"\t"$3"\t"$4"\t"$5"\t"$6}$4==\
"-"{print $1"\t"$2-1"\t"$3-1"\t"$4"\t"$5"\t"$6}' | sort -u -k1,1 -k2,2n | awk '$6>=4' > mCG_ccs.bed


# Methylation Calling and Analysis from the EM_seq dataset
# To analyze the EM-seq data, I utilized the Bisulfite Bolt (Bsbolt) pipeline. 
# This pipeline is a Python-based program and was designed to utilize forked versions of Burrow Wheelers Alignment (BWA) 
# for read alignment, and it engages the following steps of alignment, sorting, and indexing, and the actual methylation calling (Farrell et al., 2021). 
# The full description of all the necessary steps is described in Figure 8 and the lines of codes below;

# Quality check was first performed on the paired-end read using the Fastqc function in bash, then adaptors were removed using trim_galore in the line of code as; 

trim_galore -j 30 -e 0.1 -q 20 -O 1 -a -illumina ../Em_seq/Em_seq_R1 ../Em_seq/Em_seq_R2

# Indexing the reference genome

python3 -m bsbolt Index -G /path/to/ TB-assembly.posscaf.fa -DB /path/to/output

# Read alignment of the obtained trimmed Em_seq read fastq files to the indexed reference

python3 -m bsbolt Align -DB path/to/DB_output -F1 input.fastq -F1 input.fastq -O ./output/Em_meth -t 20

# Note: After filtering and alignment of the reads to the reference genome, the mapped reads accounted for approximately 99x coverage of the genome.

# Pre-processing of the aligned output file using default settings of samtools with options fixmate, 
# sort, markdup, and index to prepare the reads for duplicate removal. The reads were sorted by coordinate, 
# duplicates were removed and reads were indexed respectively. 

# Then the methylation probabilities of cytosine position were called using the code below

python3 -m bsbolt CallMethylation -I Em_dup.bam -O wgbs_out -DB ../path/to/DB_output -t 20 -verbose > Em_meth_stat

# Annotation description and genomic feature of 5-cytosine methylation detection
# Gene annotation used in this study was in-house curated by another Master’s student and I utilized only annotation of 
# scaffolds 1 through 9 as these contain the most important gene and information that drives phenotypic expression in the table beet plant. 
# The same goes for the repeat annotation which was used in this study. For further DNA methylation level and pattern analysis, 
# I utilized the gene, exon, intron, CDS, transcription start and termination site, UTRs, and the repeat information from the annotation files. 
# Then the number of methylated positions in each of these regions was assessed by overlapping the 5mC position in all three contexts (CG, CHG, CHH) 
# with methylation probability of  ≥ 20%  as truly methylated cytosine called from the different approaches to the regions of the genomic features stated above. 
# The overlapping was done using the line of command line below in bash

bedtools intersect  -f -u 1.0 -a annotation.gff3 -b 5mC_positions.bed | wc -l

# Validating and analyzing DNA methylation patterns in characteristic table beet genes (MYB1, CYP76AD1, and DODA1)
# To determine the precise coding regions of the MYB1, CYP76AD1, and DODA1 genes within the table beet reference genome under investigation, 
# relevant information pertaining to these genes and their sequences was extracted from UniProtKB. The primary accession names assigned to these 
# genes according to UniProtKB were M1ETK3, I3PFJ5.1, and I3PFJ9.1, respectively. Subsequently, the annotated coding regions were acquired in-house 
# through the following procedure: 

gffread annotation.gff3 -g TB-assembly.posscaf.fa -o output.gff -w exon.fa -x CDS.fa -y tr_CDS.fa

# Next, a BLASTp search was conducted using the translated coding sequences by aligning them against the SwissProt database. 
# The following line of code was used for this purpose:

blastp -query tr_CDS.fa -db swissprot/swissprot -out gene_last.txt -outfmt 6 -num_threads 20

# The resulting alignment output was then subjected to sorting based on percentage identity, e-value, and bit score using the following lines of code:

export LC_ALL=C LC_LANG=C;sort -k1,1 -k12,12gr -k11,11g -k3,3gr gene_last.txt > glast_sort.out;

# Collection of the top 1 BLASTp result for each gene

for i in $(cut -f1 glast_sort.out|sort -u); do grep -w -m 1 "$i" gene_last.txt; done > glast_top1.txt.

# Then leveraging the accession name information obtained from UniProtKB, the corresponding gene names in the gene annotation file were identified. 
# These aligned gene names to UniProtKB accession identifiers were used to search the annotation file containing coding sequence (CDS) information. 
# This enabled the identification of the exact CDS sequences and their corresponding information for the target genes. Subsequently, these specific CDS 
# were extracted and subjected to a BLAST alignment against the sequences derived from UniProtKB using default settings, allowing for the determination 
# of sequence similarity and identity values. Rz2 gene sequence information was obtained in-house (from other students).

# To investigate the DNA methylation patterns across all sequence contexts within the identified coding regions of the genes, 
# I employed a comprehensive methodology. Firstly, I collected the reference genome-aligned BAM files derived from two sequencing approaches, 
# along with their associated called methylation probability data, specifically obtained from the Primrose-Pb_CpG_tool and Bsbolt pipeline. 
# The focus was primarily directed toward the CDS regions of genes of interest, determined by their precise start and end coordinates. 

# Next, leveraging the available genome assembly information, I input the obtained methylation data with their corresponding annotation details 
# into the IGV browser to observe and compare the DNA methylation patterns in these genes’ coding exons and regions upstream of the transcription start sites. 

# Performance evaluation of the methylation callers (Primrose/Pb_CpG and CCS_meth)
# In order to comprehensively evaluate the performance of 5mC predictions from the two pipelines utilized in this study on the PacBio Hifi reads, 
# I down-sampled the called methylation to at least 4X coverage based on the two sequencing technologies. Then I further filtered the detected 
# cytosine methylated positions into three categories which are two representing fully methylated (consisting of positions with probabilities of ≥ 99% and ≥ 50 respectively) 
# while the other category was for fully unmethylated (consisting of positions with probabilities of ≤ 1%). Furthermore, I devised a meticulous performance evaluation framework 
# consisting of four performance metrics (Precision, Recall, F1-score, and Accuracy). Having conducted an in-depth analysis of the long-read sequencing dataset using two approaches, 
# I compared the methylated CpG sites identified from these data using the methylated CpG data obtained from EM_Seq data. In this comparison, I assessed the precision, accuracy, recall, 
# and F1 score as the performance metrics. 

The methylated CG site data from the EM_Seq data obtained from the Bsbolt pipeline was used as a truth set to benchmark the performance of the two Pacbio Hifi read methylation 
detection pipelines used in this project. Based on the EM_seq data used as a truth set, a CpG position is defined to be fully methylated with high confidence if the methylation 
probability is ≥ 99% with a coverage of at least four (4) per read for 5mC probability prediction. However, because of the genome coverage of the Hifi reads used in this research, 
I set another methylation confidence at a methylation probability of ≥ 50% and a per-read coverage of ≥ 4.  
In a similar manner, I defined a CpG position as unmethylated if the coverage was  ≥ 4 and the corresponding CpG locus had a methylation probability of ≤ 1%. To calculate these metrics, 
I employed the following formula:

• Precision: The ratio of true positive predictions to the total predicted positives, indicating the proportion of correctly predicted 5mC sites.
Precision = TP / (TP+FP)
• Accuracy: The ratio of true positive and true negative predictions to the total number of predictions, providing an overall measure of the correctness of the predictions.
Accuracy = (TP + TN) / (TP + TN + FP + FN)
• Recall: The ratio of true positive predictions to the total actual positive sites, reflecting the ability of the pipeline to identify all relevant 5mC sites.
Recall = TP / (TP + FN)
•	F1 score: The harmonic mean of precision and recall, offering a balanced assessment of the overall performance by considering both precision and recall.
F1 score = 2 × (Recall × Precision) / (Recall + Precision).

